      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When engineers encounter these unpredictable, annoying hardware errors, they often refer to them using terms like 'intermittent faults' or 'transient errors.' These are problems that don't happen all the time, making them incredibly difficult to track down, much like trying to catch a phantom. They can be caused by everything from loose connections vibrating under pressure to minute fluctuations in the power supply that are just below the threshold of a standard alarm. It's a frustrating diagnostic nightmare, as these errors are often invisible until they manifest as a spike in your data, making the data look like it was corrupted by some mischievous, tiny gremlin.",
